Scott Jones (Realeyes): On the go, and the camera, by virtue of that, will be

70
00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:26.550
Scott Jones (Realeyes): down here, it'll be over here, it'll be all over the place, and you're not getting these beautiful, beautiful images that…

71
00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:43.390
Scott Jones (Realeyes): that you might desire to run authentication. And so the idea being, we came out of that environment, and we built models that were extremely robust to handle conditions with bad lighting, weird angles, occlusions, like maybe part of your face can't even be seen.

72
00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:52.320
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Not, not pristine conditions. And then another differentiator was, going back to the very beginning and the, the genesis of the models.

73
00:07:52.450 --> 00:08:02.889
Scott Jones (Realeyes): We intentionally did not scrape data. We intentionally built an ethically sourced way to do this, and that was by designing and inventing a GDPR-compliant consent framework

74
00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:11.739
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Giving us rights to test and train models. And that ad testing business with that consent framework has generated a sort of war chest of very differentiated data

75
00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:21.849
Scott Jones (Realeyes): It's a data set of identities covering 6 million different people from more than 93 countries, and the rights to test and train models on those faces.

76
00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:39.430
Scott Jones (Realeyes): these businesses were coexisting. Since I joined three and a half years ago, we got into the identity space, we were still part of the odd testing business, but right around the very beginning of Q4 last year, we de-mergered, which is a funny word. This is, like, the fifth time I've said that word out loud, but the idea…

77
00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:51.160
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Verify and Realize, split off from the ad testing business and became our own thing, really doubling down on the identity verification and the opportunities that we found getting into this market about 3 years ago.

78
00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:59.659
Scott Jones (Realeyes): So the context of how we got in here, this was actually through an opportunity with a social media platform. We already had a relationship with them.

79
00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:04.199
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And 3 years ago, they put out an RFI for a face verification model.

80
00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:15.030
Scott Jones (Realeyes): We ended up winning their RFI, and they ended up, it took a year and a half to get in production, but as of… as of that moment, very end of 2024 and through 2025,

81
00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:29.130
Scott Jones (Realeyes): They started ramping us up to great levels, and the biggest use case was actually account recovery. So the idea being, if a user across this consumer app company's platforms can't remember their password, or they think there's been an account takeover.

82
00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:32.890
Scott Jones (Realeyes): The user has an option to submit a video selfie.

83
00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:44.470
Scott Jones (Realeyes): the consumer apps pipeline extracts frames from that selfie, known profile photos, and if you've ever shown them a government ID, the image from that government ID, not the ID itself, just the image.

84
00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:49.579
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Sends all of that to our model and essentially asks, do you think this is Scott? Should I let him in?

85
00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:54.059
Scott Jones (Realeyes): They have been rolling that out to, for lack of a better term, hyperscale

86
00:09:54.370 --> 00:10:00.140
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And for the past several months, that use case has gotten 500 million, calls per day.

87
00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:14.580
Scott Jones (Realeyes): This led us getting into this space, we were then wondering, well, alright, now we're in… now we're in the identity space. Now my platform that I was hired to run has these building blocks for not just concepts of attention and emotion, but now identity.

88
00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:22.120
Scott Jones (Realeyes): What other problems can we solve? And now that we've achieved this validation on scale that our technology can handle, and the…

89
00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:35.320
Scott Jones (Realeyes): some of the additional reasons that we won on fairness and data provenance, and I'll speak more about that. The idea that, great, now we're in this space, what other problems can we solve? And that's where we saw, that ad testing business actually gave us the target.

90
00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:52.590
Scott Jones (Realeyes): through that business, we had a front-row seat for years now to the steady rise of fraud online, so I'm referring to bots, click farms, people lying about who they are. Huge problem on the internet at large, obviously. In market research, it's truly insane. You can actually expect on the buy side, and the buyers do expect this.

91
00:10:52.880 --> 00:11:02.510
Scott Jones (Realeyes): On average, 40% of what you get, and there's really no upper bound, it can keep going, but on average, 40% of what you get will be bad, and you honestly can't tell until you buy it.

92
00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:17.280
Scott Jones (Realeyes): double-clicking on that problem in market research and then expanding to other verticals since the realizations we had are really what's described on this page. On the left side, this notion of the gap, and this idea of this continuum that has come to light, that we see.

93
00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:28.050
Scott Jones (Realeyes): So the realization that really the best thing you can do on the internet today is that heavyweight IDV check predicated on looking at a government ID, a liveness check to make sure this is a real human being.

94
00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:39.299
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And a selfie, to ascertain, yeah, real government ID belongs to Scott Jones in Chapel Hill, North Carolina. That's a real human being, yes, live in front of the camera, and I think that's the same person.

95
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:45.400
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Great for high-stakes use cases, it's got all the benefits, high assurance, it checks the compliance box.

96
00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:54.239
Scott Jones (Realeyes): But it's a lot of friction. It tends to take multiple minutes. It's quite expensive. It tends to cost a dollar more, depending on who you're using and the volume you're at.

97
00:11:54.490 --> 00:12:08.030
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And it's very invasive, and a lot of users will brush away from that churn rates for busting that out. Sorry, churn rates for presenting an IDV check in a onboarding funnel, for example, average around 40%, but can go higher.

98
00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:18.480
Scott Jones (Realeyes): So, but all to say, that's the best you can do, and if you have to do it, awesome. But for lower-stakes use cases, or kind of the connective tissue of 99% of what we do online, it's too much.

99
00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:23.530
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And if you say, okay, I don't want to do that all the time, what can I do that's less than that, that still works?

100
00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:40.149
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And that's where we see this sort of spectrum expressed on this slide. I'm keeping it simple by just referring to CAPTCHA on the left, but the left side's really inclusive of more solutions, CAPTCHA, device and network fingerprinting, bot detection, even things like SMS and email authentication, the realization we had that

101
00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:48.569
Scott Jones (Realeyes): For lack of a better way of saying it, traditional verification techniques that companies are relying on are obsolete and very challenged.

102
00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:54.259
Scott Jones (Realeyes): You can literally go on YouTube today and learn how to hack around those systems, and many people are doing it.

103
00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:06.249
Scott Jones (Realeyes): So what we capitalized on, or what we designed for, is that middle, that gap that we saw, this white space, at the intersection of being extremely easy to use, but also providing a good amount of security.

104
00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:21.439
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And what we built, it's a lightweight system, it's anonymous by design, it proves humanity, it proves uniqueness. Have you done this before? Have you onboarded to this platform before? Have you, depending on the use case, the idea of validating the uniqueness?

105
00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:32.290
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Then, confirmation of age and gender using models that we built, so being able to validate demographics. So you can't lie about who you are, but I don't need to see a government ID to prove that.

106
00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:43.440
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And it's designed with very minimal friction, I'll show you a demo in a moment, but intentional to be as seamless an experience for the end user, while still maximizing the security that we can get out of it.

107
00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:53.230
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And as I mentioned before, we already have major platforms using this with profile photos, selfie frames, and even images off of IDs, if they've already captured that.

108
00:13:53.370 --> 00:14:03.099
Scott Jones (Realeyes): So the opportunity and what we've really… what we've kind of focused on are these different use cases around re-verification, where, sure, you might do IDV up front, you have to, but…

109
00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:19.209
Scott Jones (Realeyes): a lighter weight way that can kind of fill in the gaps, so you can keep saying, yeah, this IDV check day zero told me that's Scott Jones in Chapel Hill. At the same time, I captured an embedding to verify, for example, and now I can keep checking back. Is it still that person it was day zero, and if it's never not.

110
00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.560
Scott Jones (Realeyes): You can escalate to the heavier weight check.

111
00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:39.010
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Part of the learnings we had… 2026 is the year of SMS decay. I'm kind of caffeinated right now, that's the way I'm saying it, but the idea that a lot of companies, a lot of people are shifting away from SMS because of the challenges I mentioned before.

112
00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:51.419
Scott Jones (Realeyes): not just that it's too easily hacked with SIM swapping and other modalities, but it can be very difficult. Like, it takes a lot of time, people are sick of copy and pasting. It can lead to conversion issues as well on those transactions.

113
00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:59.840
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And then the idea being, well, where are they going from SMS? And 2026 is also kind of the rise of pass keys.

114
00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:11.900
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And what we saw is pass keys do a lot of great things. Device-bound authentication, they're resistant to phishing, no password management, very, very simple, strong cryptographic binding.

115
00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:25.840
Scott Jones (Realeyes): But they have some challenges, so one big challenge is uniqueness. You can have 10 accounts on one device. You could have 10,000 devices tied to one face, so there's various ways to get past passkeys with the uniqueness challenge.

116
00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:28.339
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Cross-device continuity is another one.

117
00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:43.370
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Account recovery, if I have a passkey enabled and I lose my device, that can be very challenging. The idea of age and demographic validation, like, sure, this device is authorized, but is the person on the device the right age to have this experience they're trying to get into, for example?

118
00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:57.190
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Continuity of control, which is a big theme, and I'll tell you more about that, but this idea of being able to make sure, sure, it was the right person who validated the passkey at the start of the session, but are they still there? Is it still that person, or did they give it to someone else?

119
00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:07.860
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And then various fraud signals that can be generated. So the idea really distilling down that takeaway at the bottom, pass keys prove this device is authorized, but not necessarily it's the right person holding it.

120
00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:25.960
Scott Jones (Realeyes): And then… then this idea that another learning and opportunity that we're really leaning into is multiple verification methods, and here, distilling it down into two, but the idea, you have this very classic concept of upfront verification, so it's a gate getting into an experience.

121
00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:30.640
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Account creation and onboarding, age gate compliance, things like that.

122
00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:36.809
Scott Jones (Realeyes): generally thought of as a one-time check, where you say, cool, I checked the box, you're good to go, proceed with your session.

123
00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:49.709
Scott Jones (Realeyes): But the challenges there are many, and these are things that we see actively with the work we're doing in different markets, where, sure, that upfront check can validate it's the right person at that moment, but then they can…

124
00:16:49.750 --> 00:17:01.009
Scott Jones (Realeyes): If it's a gambling app or a gaming app, I could be a kid and have my dad look at the phone and approve me, and now I'm playing the game or the gambling that I… that I'm actually not

125
00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:17.130
Scott Jones (Realeyes): age-compliant to participate in, just as one example. Gig economy is another one where this is a huge vector of fraud. People sharing accounts, sharing credentials. They're trying to tighten the vice on it, but there is no lightweight capability they're aware of yet that can validate, sure, that was the right…

126
00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:32.100
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Uber driver, or the right delivery person at the start of the session, but is it still as they're driving the car, as they're showing up at this customer's location? And what these companies are finding is often it's not, and they're struggling to fill those gaps.

127
00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:43.270
Scott Jones (Realeyes): So this notion of continuous verification that can run in the background and make sure, validate that holder binding is still correct, and the relevance across a variety of verticals, like gig economy, gaming, gambling.

128
00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:59.349
Scott Jones (Realeyes): remote proctoring, shared workstation environments, and more. So what we've got is a spectrum of verification. We actually refer to it as progressive verification, where you can have very lightweight passive checks that can run in the background if you've already done the heavier weight one.

129
00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:04.130
Scott Jones (Realeyes): To make sure, are you still there? Are you still really a human? And are you still the right human?

130
00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:18.770
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Basic liveness that, requires very little lift from the user, but is still robust, and for those moments where you say, it's a high-stakes moment, something weird's going on, frog signals are flaring, irreversible sort of transactions, etc.

131
00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:27.880
Scott Jones (Realeyes): being able to pull the lever and say, alright, I want to do, like, the state-of-the-art, heaviest liveness check with some choreography on the user to really, really put them through it and make sure

132
00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:30.169
Scott Jones (Realeyes): That this is a, a secure moment.

133
00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:33.980
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Let me show it to you in action.

134
00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:40.170
Scott Jones (Realeyes): I believe you should still see my screen with human verification.

135
00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:52.800
Scott Jones (Realeyes): Yeah, cool. So at the bottom, you see, this is… and I can share the link after, so y'all can play with this, but at the bottom is an expression of that sort of spectrum of the verification type. So, low friction, designed to run in the background.

136
00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:59.400
Scott Jones (Realeyes): High security, which is a pretty advanced liveness check, and then balanced is a very easy liveness check, and that's the one I'll show you now.

